  "baseline_reproductions": [
    "Four added cases failed in an 18-case initial run: mutable metadata hardlinks admitted; configured commit identity masked; helper-enabling options admitted; a configured editor was not refused.",
    "A synthetic Git timeout left its descendant able to write later. The deterministic marker regression failed before shared supervision.",
    "An activated nested-submodule pointer triggered native recursive checkout against unadmitted metadata; early incomplete fixture variants were corrected. No completed outside write or general submodule qualification is claimed.",
    "Nested outer-first shutdown, inactive/valueless worktree config, failure aggregation and default macOS temp-path admission were follow-up source-review findings with fresh passing controls."
  ],
  "baseline_evidence_custody": "Fresh synthetic failures are in task tool output; no separately retained raw baseline log or recovered blocked report is claimed.",
  "corrections": [
    "Admit explicit owned disposable roots, gitdirs, common dirs and indexes; clear inherited Git redirects/config and disable configured helpers.",
    "Reject mutable metadata hardlinks while preserving actual immutable loose/pack object sharing used by native local clone.",
    "Admit explicit noninteractive fixture arguments; refuse helper, signing, recursion and authority overrides before effects. This is fixture-only, not a public Git CLI wrapper.",
    "Preserve configured commit identity and native active-worktree Boolean semantics, including valueless/nonzero values.",
    "Native Git and Node use shared supervision. Instrumented nested Node workers inherit one outer group; timeout/signal escalates to its owner. Only that external owner may clean after group absence is proved.",
    "Upgrade fixture cleanup uses exact minted-root identity, aggregates failures and reports success only afterward. Internal OS temp aliases canonicalize; explicit overrides remain subject to admission.",
    "Three installed qualification callers use the adapter; two executed installed families passed locally."
  ],
